{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\n\nMake the bridge execution path portable across local shells, GitHub Actions, and Jenkins without\nbreaking checked-out workflows or environments that still use the legacy reconcile vocabulary.\nThe current production workflow contains about 185 lines of runner logic, and\n`src/rebar/_commands/fsck_repair.py` shells out to `gh workflow disable/enable` without a repository\nargument. That best-effort GitHub-only schedule control silently disappears on Jenkins.\n\nThe bridge/reconciler execution path is otherwise CI-neutral. The repository as a whole is not:\n`src/rebar/mirror_guard.py` intentionally reads `GITHUB_TOKEN`, hard-codes the GitHub mirror, and\ncalls `api.github.com`; `src/rebar/llm/usage_log.py` and `usage_report.py` optionally write\n`GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY`. Those are GitHub-specific mirror-health/reporting features, not bridge\nexecution, and are documented out of scope rather than used as evidence of bridge portability.\n\nGitLab is also out of scope. There is no current `.gitlab-ci.yml`, runner, or consumer, so shipping a\nfourth unexercised provider configuration would create the same rot surface this story removes.\nFuture GitLab adoption can reuse the runner-neutral entrypoint, but no unrun GitLab config or schema\nvalidator ships here.\n\n## Runner-neutral entrypoint\n\nCreate `scripts/run_reconcile_bridge.py` as the one executable bridge wrapper. It selects only the\ncanonical `rebar bridge preview` or `rebar bridge sync` operation, owns the canonical wrapper\ncontract (`CANONICAL_EXIT_CODES`, required environment names, and timeout), rejects shallow clones,\nand contains no GitHub/Jenkins branch. `.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml`, `Jenkinsfile`, and\nlocal documentation invoke that exact file. Provider wrappers retain only checkout, credentials,\ntimeout, artifact/masking syntax, and the platform-specific expression that maps the entrypoint exit\nstatus to job success/failure.\n\nThe entrypoint calls the checkout build and canonical bridge route. Legacy `rebar reconcile --mode`\nand direct-engine routes remain untouched, including their 3/4/75 results, so older workflow\nrevisions and environments continue to run during migration.\n\n`scripts/run_reconcile_bridge.py` is the wrapper source of truth for the secret-name set and timeout.\nIts allowed exits are derived and cross-checked against\n`src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/__main__.py::_Disposition`, whose canonical column is 0/1/2.\nContract tests import the runner constants, parse each provider wrapper, and compare them; the tests\ndo not restate a second table.\n\n## Portable destructive-repair control\n\nReplace the `gh workflow disable/enable` helpers with the durable control already shipped by story\n`1104`: `refs/reconciler/gate` pause documents written through\n`rebar_reconciler._ref_lock.set_pause` and cleared through `clear_gate`, using the same configured\nremote and identity behavior as `rebar bridge pause/resume`. A scheduled pass on GitHub or Jenkins\nreads that ref before mutation and exits benignly while paused.\n\nThe repair sequence is explicit and fail-closed:\n\n1. CAS-write the durable pause ref. Any transport, authentication, corruption, or CAS uncertainty\n   aborts the destructive repair.\n2. Run the existing provider-neutral `_reconciler_in_flight` check against\n   `refs/reconciler/lock`. HELD, corrupt, timeout, or indeterminate aborts the repair.\n3. Run the repair.\n4. Clear only the pause written/observed by this repair in `finally`; a resume failure is surfaced\n   and leaves the bridge paused for operator recovery rather than silently reopening it.\n\nThe lock remains the in-flight safety check; the durable gate is the schedule pause. This preserves\nthe currently-working GitHub safety behavior without a provider API and closes the Jenkins race.\n\n## Verification strategy\n\n- Run the real entrypoint in temporary repositories with vendor variables scrubbed and with\n  representative local, GitHub Actions, Jenkins, and bare environments. Results and repository/ref\n  postconditions must be identical, and `gh` is absent from `PATH`.\n- Use a bare git remote to set the durable pause, attempt a canonical sync, and prove the pass exits\n  benignly without mutation. Exercise pause write failure, corrupt/indeterminate lock, and resume\n  failure and prove destructive repair fails closed.\n- Create a real shallow clone (or drive `git rev-parse --is-shallow-repository` to `true`) and assert\n  the entrypoint exits non-zero with the named `f193` error before any bridge operation runs.\n- Parse the production GHA and Jenkins wrappers and execute their extracted shell bodies. The\n  Jenkins `sh` body is a single-quoted Groovy string so secrets reach the shell/masking layer without\n  GString interpolation.\n- `shellcheck` is a pinned CI/dev prerequisite installed by the verification workflow. The contract\n  test hard-fails with a setup error when the binary is missing; it never skips. A malformed wrapper\n  fixture must make extraction/lint fail.\n- The production GHA workflow is still the scheduled artifact rebar runs, so the shared entrypoint is\n  exercised by the existing 20-minute schedule. No Jenkins controller is stood up: the committed\n  Jenkinsfile uses the same extracted-body and contract checks.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `scripts/run_reconcile_bridge.py` is the one runner-neutral bridge entrypoint; GHA and Jenkins\n      wrappers contain only checkout, credentials, timeout, masking/artifacts, and exit mapping.\n- [ ] Checked-out legacy workflows and old-vocabulary environments retain the legacy reconcile\n      commands, defaults, messages, and 3/4/75 behavior.\n- [ ] `fsck --repair` uses the durable pause ref plus the fail-closed lock check and requires no `gh`\n      binary; pause/lock/resume uncertainty never permits a destructive repair to proceed silently.\n- [ ] A real bare-remote test proves the durable pause blocks canonical sync identically from local,\n      GHA-shaped, and Jenkins-shaped environments.\n- [ ] A contract matrix runs the real entrypoint under scrubbed local/GHA/Jenkins/bare environments,\n      with `gh` absent, and asserts identical exit/output/ref effects and no vendor-variable reads.\n- [ ] A shallow-clone test drives the true shallow condition and asserts the named `f193` error and\n      non-zero exit before reconciliation.\n- [ ] The shipped GHA artifact invokes the shared entrypoint and remains the workflow exercised on\n      the existing schedule; a committed `Jenkinsfile` invokes the same entrypoint.\n- [ ] Provider wrappers are cross-checked against the runner constants and `_Disposition` canonical\n      exits, never against duplicated hard-coded tables.\n- [ ] Jenkins shell bodies are single-quoted; pinned `shellcheck` runs in CI, fails when unavailable,\n      and rejects malformed extracted script/config fixtures.\n- [ ] GitHub-only mirror/report helpers are documented as out of bridge scope; GitLab support is an\n      accepted follow-up only when a real consumer exists.\n\n## File impact\n\n- `scripts/run_reconcile_bridge.py`: runner-neutral entrypoint and wrapper contract.\n- `.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml`: thin production GHA wrapper over the entrypoint.\n- `.github/workflows/_build-and-test.yml`: install/pin shellcheck for the contract gate.\n- `Jenkinsfile`: thin committed Jenkins wrapper with safe credential interpolation.\n- `src/rebar/_commands/fsck_repair.py`: durable pause/ref-lock repair sequence; no `gh` subprocess.\n- focused workflow/runner/fsck tests: environment parity, shallow rejection, shell extraction, and\n  pause/lock/resume fail-closed behavior.\n- `docs/user-guide.md` and `docs/release-notes.md`: local/GHA/Jenkins usage, legacy continuity,\n  GitHub-specific known gaps, and accepted GitLab deferral."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786291381079554001,"uuid":"fe0d25d0-0897-4ac1-bffc-e26ca0e05e9f"}